## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to apply concrete Google Cloud controls for multi-tenant isolation at runtime, beyond theoretical concepts, with a practical path from IAM to data and storage gates.

## Key Concepts

- IAM Conditions for per-tenant API access
- Storage prefixes and signed URLs for per-tenant data
- Per-tenant logging/alerting for exfiltration attempts
- CI gates that detect misconfig and roll back changes

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple tenant check in API middleware
function authorize(req, res, next) {
  const tenantFromToken = req.user?.tenantId;
  const requestedTenant = req.params.tenantId;
  if (tenantFromToken !== requestedTenant) return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the CI gate if a misconfig slips through?
- What are trade-offs of per-tenant prefixes vs separate buckets for storage isolation?

## Why This Is Asked
This question validates expertise in PKI design, mTLS implementation, and CI/CD containment strategies for multi-tenant SaaS architectures.

## Key Concepts
- Private CA hierarchies per tenant for isolation
- mTLS with SPIFFE/SVID for workload identity
- Automated certificate rotation and revocation handling
- CI validation and rollback playbooks
- Observability via Cloud Monitoring, Cloud Logging, and Cloud IDS

## Code Example
```javascript
// CI gate: verify issued certificates include tenantId in SAN
function validateCert(certPem, tenantId) {
  const cert = new X509.Cert(certPem);
  const sans = cert.getSubjectAltName();
  return sans.includes(`tenantId:${tenantId}`);
}

// Validate certificate chain integrity
function validateChain(chainPem) {
  const chain = X509.CertChain.fromPEM(chainPem);
  return chain.verify();
}
```

The code above demonstrates CI-level validation: the first function ensures each certificate contains the tenant identifier in its Subject Alternative Names, enabling tenant-aware access control. The second function verifies the entire certificate chain signature integrity before deployment, preventing compromised or tampered certificates from entering the production mesh.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical implementation of per-tenant isolation in a shared GCP project, focusing on real-world controls rather than theory.

## Key Concepts
- IAM Conditions and least-privilege service accounts
- VPC Service Controls and Private Service Connect
- Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK)
- CI/CD gates with synthetic tenants and rollback safety
- Data Loss Prevention (DLP) and ongoing IAM reviews

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo snippet: enforce tenant-scoped sink bindings
const sink = getSink("bigquery-export");
bindPolicy(sink, {
  condition: {
    title: "tenant_scope",
    expression: 'resource.name.startsWith("projects/PROJ/datasets/tenant-")'
  },
  bindings: [{ role: "roles/bigquery.dataEditor", members: ["serviceAccount:tenant-a-sa@proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com"] }]
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt the controls for a new tenant with stricter data residency requirements?
- How would you measure and report control effectiveness to audits?
